  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 940 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another batch of rain expected overnight and through \nSaturday, with the highest rain totals across NE NC. Highs \nSaturday remain below normal, mainly in the 70s to near 80.\n\nLatest WX analysis indicates a nearly stationary draped across \ncoastal Carolinas, with westerly flow aloft. A low amplitude  \nupper level trough is in place from the mid-South into the\nEastern Great Lakes/interior northeast, and is forecast to \namplify to the west of the Appalachians overnight, eventually \ndropping SE across the region on Saturday. The flow aloft looks \nto back slightly later tonight, to the WSW, helping to lift the \nmoisture and precip back north into the local area. Already\nnoting some light showers pushing across the piedmont early this\nevening, with CAMs showing likely to categorical PoPs into \nsouth central VA and NE NC after midnight. The models also \nsupport raising PoPs to 40-60% as far north as metro RIC, though\nrainfall amounts will tend to average 0.10-0.20\" or less that \nfar to the north, typical with this weak CAD pattern. Meanwhile, \nQPF amounts over far southern VA and NE NC will average 0.50\" to\n1.00\" with some locally higher amounts possible in NE NC. \nThunderstorms look quite limited north of the VA- NC border, but\nwill bring slight chances into far southeastern VA for in case \na few elevated cells develop where the NAM/GFS depict ~200 J/Kg \nof MU CAPE late tonight into Sat aftn. \n\nRain chances and rather cool high temperatures continue \nSaturday, especially south. Lows tonight will mostly be in the \n60s with some lower 70s in NE NC and at the coast in SE VA. \nHighs Saturday will range from the mid to upper 70s across the \nsouth, with some lower 80s NE (where some partial sun is most \nlikely). Drying out Sat night as the upper trough moves \noffshore, with lows mainly in the 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with just isolated late day showers/storms\nSunday/Monday, with higher rain chances Tuesday into Wednesday;\nstrong to severe storms are possible Tuesday. \n\n\nWeak sfc high pressure is forecast to settle over the region\nSunday-Monday, bringing a return to more seasonable temperatures\nwith highs in the mid 80s to around 90F. Mainly dry outside of\nisolated to widely scattered late day convection. Storm chances\nincrease later Tuesday as a strong upper level trough dives SE \nfrom Canada and lingers across the NE and mid-Atlantic states \nthrough at least Wednesday. The best chance for widespread \nstorms appears to be late Tuesday afternoon/evening though with \na lot of uncertainty at this range. With the strength of trough,\nand even a potential sfc low <1000mb, there will be the \npotential for severe weather, especially if the timing lines up\nwith the greatest instability, and SPC Day 5 Outlook has the \nlocal area in a Slight Risk.",
